WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.909
Film Courage: Ken, I have a quote here from
the late author Phillip Roth.

00:00:03.909 --> 00:00:07.600
And the quote is “The road to hell is paved
with works-in-progress.”

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:14.139
Dr. Ken Atchity, Author/Producer: It’s worse
than good intentions right?

00:00:14.139 --> 00:00:15.139
And was that a question?

00:00:15.139 --> 00:00:18.340
Film Courage: It’s a statement and a question
I guess?

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:20.110
Do you have any ideas on this?

00:00:20.110 --> 00:00:22.380
I know it’s kind of just a thing I’m throwing
out here.

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:28.740
Ken: Well I think unfinished works are the
kind of characteristic debris of the writer’s

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:29.740
life.

00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:35.360
Of any visionary’s life I mean the discards
that Leonardo DaVinci had in his studio were

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:41.820
prodigious and you just have a lot of ideas
and you can’t do them all.

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:48.710
And one of the methods that I’ve developed
over the years is to actually set aside a

00:00:48.710 --> 00:00:56.420
new idea and give it a two week rest and check
in with it in 2 weeks to see if you even remember

00:00:56.420 --> 00:00:59.050
the idea.

00:00:59.050 --> 00:01:02.170
And the key to doing that as a writer is not
to write it down.

00:01:02.170 --> 00:01:08.990
See it’s like a basic rule that I have is
that if you have an idea and as a writer your

00:01:08.990 --> 00:01:13.620
immediate goal is to write it down (get it
down) because that’s what writers do, if

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:18.050
you can train your brain not to do that you
are going to have much better product in the

00:01:18.050 --> 00:01:23.520
long run because you are not writing every
single thing down including every bad thing.

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:30.640
So by not writing it down when you revisit
it in 2 weeks from now, if you don’t remember

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:31.880
it, that’s great.

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:37.940
That means one lousy idea went away as opposed
to trying to do something with every single

00:01:37.940 --> 00:01:40.560
idea that you have.

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:46.940
So I think that’s part of what creative
people learn is how to manage their own minds

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:53.420
and because I was an accountant’s son I
long ago analyzed the creative process, the

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:59.150
creative mind and decided that they weren’t
just crazy (the way a lot of people think).

00:01:59.150 --> 00:02:05.450
There actually is a method to creativity and
in fact it spreads across every discipline

00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:13.790
whether you are a physicist or a mathematician
or an inventor or a writer or an artist, the

00:02:13.790 --> 00:02:17.940
creative process has the same steps and the
same general pattern.

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:25.311
So if you can understand the process you aren’t
nearly as neurotic as if you don’t understand

00:02:25.311 --> 00:02:27.470
it.

00:02:27.470 --> 00:02:31.160
One of my goals was to not be crazy.

00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:36.720
Salvador Dali said (one of my favorite things
about this is) he said the different between

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:41.580
this and madman is that I am not mad.

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:46.340
And I love that because it’s exactly what
I am talking about, it’s understanding the

00:02:46.340 --> 00:02:49.790
method in your madness as Shakespeare put
it.

00:02:49.790 --> 00:02:54.930
If you can understand that then you don’t
have to be unhappy or neurotic in order to

00:02:54.930 --> 00:02:56.319
be a productive writer.

00:02:56.319 --> 00:03:00.160
Film Courage: Speaking of which (writing things
down), do you make lists?

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:05.770
Ken: I do make lists but kind of limited.

00:03:05.770 --> 00:03:11.849
I mean what I have instead is a very complicated
method of time management that involves a

00:03:11.849 --> 00:03:18.360
charge that I make regularly and the chart
has room for some lists but most of what I

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:25.690
do is doing the same things in compartments
of time that I believe are the right compartments

00:03:25.690 --> 00:03:26.730
for what I do.

00:03:26.730 --> 00:03:31.180
Film Courage: I know in our last interview
which was about a year (or a year and a half

00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:36.959
ago) we talked about one of your books and
we discussed time management and that creative

00:03:36.959 --> 00:03:40.240
people who get things done are very aware
of time.

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:42.170
So then I began to monitor myself.

00:03:42.170 --> 00:03:45.590
Am I really as aware of time as I should be?

00:03:45.590 --> 00:03:51.690
Maybe I am not aware enough and so it becomes
this new thing where (you were talking about

00:03:51.690 --> 00:03:56.500
this before) that most people who are productive,
they know exactly how much time something

00:03:56.500 --> 00:03:58.129
takes them.

00:03:58.129 --> 00:04:02.620
And this was interesting because I thought
I was aware of time but then when I started

00:04:02.620 --> 00:04:06.890
listening to that I realized no I’m actually
not because I don’t know how much time [something

00:04:06.890 --> 00:04:13.210
takes me] and it ends up where I’m not giving
myself enough of it.

00:04:13.210 --> 00:04:16.979
Can someone be too aware of time where it
becomes a hindrance?

00:04:16.979 --> 00:04:24.240
Ken: Yes I think it’s possible in today’s
world with all of the watches and technology

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:27.590
and devices for keeping track of time.

00:04:27.590 --> 00:04:33.449
And then a very separate subject but it’s
very connected with creativity because time

00:04:33.449 --> 00:04:34.449
is all we have.

00:04:34.449 --> 00:04:40.150
I mean there are two things in life you can
manage, one is work and one is time.

00:04:40.150 --> 00:04:46.099
And one of them is infinite and the other
one is finite.

00:04:46.099 --> 00:04:53.080
So without even talking further about it,
you think about that a realize that by definition

00:04:53.080 --> 00:04:56.330
you cannot manage an infinite thing right?

00:04:56.330 --> 00:04:57.500
An infinite element.

00:04:57.500 --> 00:05:02.259
An infinite element cannot be managed, but
a finite one can.

00:05:02.259 --> 00:05:08.809
But for some reason along the way as we grow
up in the world we think that the wrong one

00:05:08.809 --> 00:05:11.309
is infinite and we think it’s time.

00:05:11.309 --> 00:05:13.379
And it’s not true.

00:05:13.379 --> 00:05:17.470
The only one that is time is infinite for
is God.

00:05:17.470 --> 00:05:20.550
For the rest of us, it’s all too finite.

00:05:20.550 --> 00:05:23.430
But what is infinite is work.

00:05:23.430 --> 00:05:30.099
Work is completely infinite because good work
produces more work.

00:05:30.099 --> 00:05:32.759
You know as my son once told me, Dad you’ll
never catch up.

00:05:32.759 --> 00:05:35.389
I was telling him “I really hope I can catch
up this weekend.”

00:05:35.389 --> 00:05:37.919
And he goes “You are never going to catch
up.”

00:05:37.919 --> 00:05:40.870
And he’s right because work is infinite.

00:05:40.870 --> 00:05:43.490
If it’s good work, it generates more work.

00:05:43.490 --> 00:05:46.770
If it’s bad work, it generates more work.

00:05:46.770 --> 00:05:49.440
So no matter how you look at it work is infinite.

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:51.369
You can’t manage work.

00:05:51.369 --> 00:05:53.479
You can only manage time.

00:05:53.479 --> 00:06:02.490
And you can manage time if you know how to
compartmentalize it in a productive way that

00:06:02.490 --> 00:06:04.809
works with your particular mind.

00:06:04.809 --> 00:06:11.909
What I mean by that is I think that the first
step in managing time other than keeping track

00:06:11.909 --> 00:06:17.039
of your time like you were talking about,
when I gave classes on that and when I’m

00:06:17.039 --> 00:06:21.759
consulting with individuals about their time
management, we start by having them make a

00:06:21.759 --> 00:06:24.919
weekly charge of their time.

00:06:24.919 --> 00:06:28.530
You ask people how many hours are there in
a week and they don’t even know because

00:06:28.530 --> 00:06:30.580
that just never occurred to them.

00:06:30.580 --> 00:06:35.259
But there is a finite number of hours in every
week and what I want to know first is what

00:06:35.259 --> 00:06:37.719
do you do with those hours?

00:06:37.719 --> 00:06:46.139
Exactly how many hours do you spend sleeping,
eating, walking, exercising, talking on the

00:06:46.139 --> 00:06:54.120
phone, texting, emailing and working on the
things you are supposed to work on.

00:06:54.120 --> 00:06:57.920
And doing errands and doing all of the other
things that you don’t really want to do

00:06:57.920 --> 00:07:00.740
but you kind of have to do to be human.

00:07:00.740 --> 00:07:06.189
So once you know that the next step is to
figure out attention span.

00:07:06.189 --> 00:07:10.399
Because when I was a professor I would come
and say they were failing history and they

00:07:10.399 --> 00:07:13.629
didn’t get it because they were spending
six hours a day studying history.

00:07:13.629 --> 00:07:16.419
And I’d go “Wait a minute, six hours a
day?”

00:07:16.419 --> 00:07:18.650
“Yes, because I’m failing in it.”

00:07:18.650 --> 00:07:23.360
And I’d go “Well, it’s very possible
that you’re spending too much time, not

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:25.210
too little time.”

00:07:25.210 --> 00:07:31.809
Because what happens during those six hours
is probably not the most productive way of

00:07:31.809 --> 00:07:36.999
studying history and we would rearrange their
patterns so that they would actually study

00:07:36.999 --> 00:07:41.839
history only one hour a day but do it in an
interrupted way.

00:07:41.839 --> 00:07:45.949
And here’s what you do during that hour,
etc.

00:07:45.949 --> 00:07:50.699
And so what we’re trying to figure out is
what is your attention span for an individual

00:07:50.699 --> 00:07:51.699
subject.

00:07:51.699 --> 00:07:59.009
So if we know that this person can pay attention
to history for one hour and after that her

00:07:59.009 --> 00:08:05.120
mind starts wandering, then it’s a complete
waste of time literally to spend more than

00:08:05.120 --> 00:08:07.780
one hour studying history at a time.

00:08:07.780 --> 00:08:10.599
That’s what I call a compartment of time.

00:08:10.599 --> 00:08:17.309
If it comes to your writing, how much time
can you write being fully focused and not

00:08:17.309 --> 00:08:19.610
thinking about the outside world, etc.

00:08:19.610 --> 00:08:25.990
And that’s the compartment where your attention
span is at its max, because if you’re doing

00:08:25.990 --> 00:08:31.419
anything where your attention span is not
at its max you are basically wasting your

00:08:31.419 --> 00:08:39.180
time and your energy and both of those things
have negative, kind of depressive effect on

00:08:39.180 --> 00:08:42.890
your motivation, they are not good.

00:08:42.890 --> 00:08:47.530
So you really want to figure out your attention
span and then you want to arrange your life

00:08:47.530 --> 00:08:53.530
in compartments of time that have to do with
attention span.

00:08:53.530 --> 00:08:59.561
And when it comes to being conscious of time
one of my rules has been from the very beginning

00:08:59.561 --> 00:09:04.500
the first time I started thinking of these
things when I was like 19 is that I stopped

00:09:04.500 --> 00:09:06.130
wearing a watch.

00:09:06.130 --> 00:09:11.371
Because I realized that the only way I was
going to be productive with my own interest

00:09:11.371 --> 00:09:18.250
and activities is if I lived in my own time
and did not live in everybody else’s time.

00:09:18.250 --> 00:09:22.510
And everywhere you look there is a clock on
the wall, there’s Big Ben on the horizon,

00:09:22.510 --> 00:09:27.990
there’s television monitor’s with countdowns
on them.

00:09:27.990 --> 00:09:31.640
There is everything out there reminding you
of the world’s time.

00:09:31.640 --> 00:09:34.900
And the world’s time is not your time.

00:09:34.900 --> 00:09:38.460
You don’t have all the time in the world,
you have your own time.

00:09:38.460 --> 00:09:43.680
So I discovered a method year’s ago which
is simply the stop watch method.

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:46.910
Which is instead of using clocks you use a
stop watch.

00:09:46.910 --> 00:09:51.680
And you tell yourself (for example) I’m
going to write for an hour and a half today

00:09:51.680 --> 00:09:56.200
no matter what and I’m going to monitor
that on a stop watch.

00:09:56.200 --> 00:10:00.150
And I will turn the stop watch on when I am
actually writing.

00:10:00.150 --> 00:10:03.640
And when I am not writing like if the phone
rings and I have to take it or if the house

00:10:03.640 --> 00:10:08.920
burns down and I have to deal with that, then
I will turn off the stop watch until that

00:10:08.920 --> 00:10:11.040
particular interruption is over with.

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:14.770
And then I’ll go back until I get my hour
and a half on the stop watch.

00:10:14.770 --> 00:10:21.310
And it doesn’t matter whether you are doing
it at 3:00 p.m. or 3:00 a.m. or 8:00 p.m.,

00:10:21.310 --> 00:10:22.310
etc.

00:10:22.310 --> 00:10:27.570
As long as you get that hour and a half on
the stop watch you are in good shape.

00:10:27.570 --> 00:10:32.300
So sometimes I have three or four stop watches
around depending on what project I’m applying

00:10:32.300 --> 00:10:36.820
them to and of course I’ve got my computer
stop watch.

00:10:36.820 --> 00:10:42.270
And I rarely look at the time except if there’s
an appointment or something I have to be aware

00:10:42.270 --> 00:10:48.300
of because I’m really focused on my time
which is the stop watches time and that’s

00:10:48.300 --> 00:10:54.160
what I need to be focused on if want to be
in that unique category of people who create

00:10:54.160 --> 00:10:57.620
things (in my case, manage people who create
things).

